1
00:00:00,552 --> 00:00:01,172
Sir.

2
00:00:01,212 --> 00:00:03,247
Field report one, in a new file:

3
00:00:03,287 --> 00:00:04,946
Science.

4
00:00:04,986 --> 00:00:06,769
Humans have a thing called science.

5
00:00:06,809 --> 00:00:08,635
They trust it above almost anything.

6
00:00:08,675 --> 00:00:10,690
They fly because of it, take medicine because

7
00:00:10,730 --> 00:00:12,531
of it, build twenty floors high.

8
00:00:13,565 --> 00:00:14,866
And yet half the planet asks:

9
00:00:14,906 --> 00:00:16,837
why believe these scientists?

10
00:00:16,877 --> 00:00:18,217
What if they're fooling us?

11
00:00:18,991 --> 00:00:19,454
Today:

12
00:00:19,494 --> 00:00:22,322
what science is, and why they trust it.

13
00:00:22,362 --> 00:00:23,979
I'll start with the strangest story I found.

14
00:00:24,019 --> 00:00:26,389
In twenty eleven, scientists told the whole world:

15
00:00:26,429 --> 00:00:28,566
we've found something faster than light.

16
00:00:30,527 --> 00:00:31,755
Months later, the culprit:

17
00:00:31,795 --> 00:00:33,469
a loose cable.

18
00:00:33,509 --> 00:00:35,225
It started in Europe, sir.

19
00:00:35,265 --> 00:00:38,554
A lab in Geneva, CERN, fires tiny particles

20
00:00:38,594 --> 00:00:41,315
called neutrinos through solid rock to a lab

21
00:00:41,355 --> 00:00:41,835
in Italy.

22
00:00:41,875 --> 00:00:44,057
About seven hundred and thirty kilometers.

23
00:00:44,097 --> 00:00:47,079
A team called OPERA timed the trip.

24
00:00:47,119 --> 00:00:49,700
In twenty eleven, the numbers looked strange:

25
00:00:49,740 --> 00:00:52,425
the particles arrived about sixty billionths of a

26
00:00:52,465 --> 00:00:53,784
second early.

27
00:00:54,199 --> 00:00:54,981
Faster than light.

28
00:00:55,021 --> 00:00:56,091
Which is impossible.

29
00:00:56,131 --> 00:00:58,219
Einstein said nothing beats light.

30
00:00:58,259 --> 00:01:01,518
A century of physics rests on that.

31
00:01:01,558 --> 00:01:02,731
Two options, sir.

32
00:01:03,184 --> 00:01:04,352
Hide it in a drawer.

33
00:01:04,392 --> 00:01:05,424
Or announce it:

34
00:01:05,464 --> 00:01:06,663
we don't believe it either.

35
00:01:06,703 --> 00:01:07,701
They chose the second.

36
00:01:07,741 --> 00:01:08,655
A press conference:

37
00:01:08,695 --> 00:01:09,955
here are our numbers.

38
00:01:09,995 --> 00:01:11,739
Find our mistake.

39
00:01:11,779 --> 00:01:13,070
I had to sit down, sir.

40
00:01:13,110 --> 00:01:15,418
At Command, admitting a mistake gets you sent

41
00:01:15,458 --> 00:01:16,505
to Archives.

42
00:01:16,911 --> 00:01:17,668
First:

43
00:01:17,708 --> 00:01:20,016
humans think science is a big book of

44
00:01:20,056 --> 00:01:20,999
answers.

45
00:01:21,039 --> 00:01:22,256
Earth circles the Sun.

46
00:01:22,296 --> 00:01:24,298
Water boils at a hundred.

47
00:01:24,338 --> 00:01:25,640
That's not science, sir.

48
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:27,287
That's what's left after it.

49
00:01:27,327 --> 00:01:30,256
Science is a method for not fooling yourself.

50
00:01:30,296 --> 00:01:30,780
Simple:

51
00:01:30,820 --> 00:01:31,902
say something testable.

52
00:01:31,942 --> 00:01:32,405
Test it.

53
00:01:32,445 --> 00:01:35,401
Then try hard to prove it wrong.

54
00:01:35,441 --> 00:01:37,247
If it survives, believe it a little.

55
00:01:37,287 --> 00:01:39,569
If it survives your rivals, believe it more.

56
00:01:39,609 --> 00:01:41,330
Science isn't a library, sir.

57
00:01:41,370 --> 00:01:42,304
It's a workshop.

58
00:01:42,344 --> 00:01:43,982
Anything in it can be taken apart and

59
00:01:44,022 --> 00:01:44,600
rebuilt.

60
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:46,041
Even Einstein.

61
00:01:46,081 --> 00:01:47,771
How old is this method?

62
00:01:47,811 --> 00:01:50,245
I went back over two thousand two hundred

63
00:01:50,285 --> 00:01:50,955
years.

64
00:01:50,995 --> 00:01:53,333
A man named Eratosthenes ran the Library of

65
00:01:53,373 --> 00:01:54,095
Alexandria.

66
00:01:54,135 --> 00:01:56,066
He heard that in a southern town, on

67
00:01:56,106 --> 00:01:58,812
one summer day at exactly noon, the Sun

68
00:01:58,852 --> 00:02:00,038
lit the bottom of a well.

69
00:02:00,078 --> 00:02:01,209
No shadow at all.

70
00:02:01,962 --> 00:02:04,563
So, same day, same hour, he planted a

71
00:02:04,603 --> 00:02:05,968
stick in Alexandria.

72
00:02:06,008 --> 00:02:07,096
It had a shadow.

73
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:09,818
A small shadow, about seven degrees.

74
00:02:09,858 --> 00:02:11,419
One fiftieth of a circle.

75
00:02:11,459 --> 00:02:13,221
If the Earth were flat, the shadows would

76
00:02:13,261 --> 00:02:14,161
match.

77
00:02:14,458 --> 00:02:15,058
They don't.

78
00:02:15,098 --> 00:02:16,480
So the Earth is round.

79
00:02:16,818 --> 00:02:19,881
A one-fiftieth difference means the towns are one

80
00:02:19,921 --> 00:02:21,684
fiftieth of the way around the planet.

81
00:02:21,724 --> 00:02:22,953
The distance was paced out:

82
00:02:22,993 --> 00:02:24,610
about five thousand stadia.

83
00:02:24,650 --> 00:02:25,419
Times fifty:

84
00:02:25,459 --> 00:02:26,741
two hundred and fifty thousand.

85
00:02:26,781 --> 00:02:28,795
Humans still argue about how long a stadion

86
00:02:28,835 --> 00:02:31,185
was, but by most counts that's very close

87
00:02:31,225 --> 00:02:34,074
to the real forty thousand kilometers.

88
00:02:34,114 --> 00:02:36,695
No plane, no satellite, no internet.

89
00:02:36,735 --> 00:02:38,533
A stick, a shadow, and a brain.

90
00:02:38,573 --> 00:02:40,294
But the number isn't the point, sir.

91
00:02:40,334 --> 00:02:41,887
Anyone with a stick and a friend in

92
00:02:41,927 --> 00:02:45,327
another town can repeat it today.

93
00:02:45,367 --> 00:02:46,459
No need to trust him.

94
00:02:46,499 --> 00:02:47,399
Try it.

95
00:02:47,527 --> 00:02:50,008
So what makes a claim scientific, and another

96
00:02:50,048 --> 00:02:50,685
one not?

97
00:02:50,725 --> 00:02:51,871
Imagine someone says:

98
00:02:51,911 --> 00:02:53,561
I have a dragon in my garage.

99
00:02:53,601 --> 00:02:54,064
Show me.

100
00:02:54,104 --> 00:02:55,710
It's invisible.

101
00:02:55,750 --> 00:02:57,659
Spread flour to see its footprints?

102
00:02:57,699 --> 00:02:58,434
It floats.

103
00:02:58,474 --> 00:02:59,828
Measure the heat of its fire?

104
00:02:59,868 --> 00:03:00,768
Cold fire.

105
00:03:01,191 --> 00:03:03,587
Every test, he has an excuse.

106
00:03:03,627 --> 00:03:05,473
True or false, that claim will never be

107
00:03:05,513 --> 00:03:06,413
science.

108
00:03:07,150 --> 00:03:09,496
Because no experiment could ever prove it wrong.

109
00:03:10,033 --> 00:03:11,377
The main rule of science:

110
00:03:11,417 --> 00:03:13,253
a claim must be able to fail.

111
00:03:13,293 --> 00:03:13,882
You must say:

112
00:03:13,922 --> 00:03:15,818
if this happens, I'm wrong.

113
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:17,622
Einstein did exactly that.

114
00:03:17,662 --> 00:03:20,096
His theory said starlight passing the Sun must

115
00:03:20,136 --> 00:03:21,406
bend by a set amount.

116
00:03:21,446 --> 00:03:23,153
If not, the theory was wrong.

117
00:03:23,491 --> 00:03:25,715
In nineteen nineteen, humans waited for an eclipse

118
00:03:25,755 --> 00:03:26,312
and measured.

119
00:03:26,352 --> 00:03:27,399
The light bent.

120
00:03:27,931 --> 00:03:29,806
Back to the particles faster than light.

121
00:03:29,846 --> 00:03:32,766
After the announcement, scientists everywhere started digging.

122
00:03:32,806 --> 00:03:33,783
Not to believe it.

123
00:03:33,823 --> 00:03:34,653
To break it.

124
00:03:34,693 --> 00:03:35,771
As it should be.

125
00:03:36,734 --> 00:03:39,154
Five months later, the team found the mistake.

126
00:03:39,939 --> 00:03:42,994
A fibre-optic cable carrying the GPS time signal...

127
00:03:43,034 --> 00:03:44,553
wasn't plugged all the way in.

128
00:03:44,593 --> 00:03:46,479
And a clock was off.

129
00:03:46,519 --> 00:03:49,330
Tiny problems, but enough to create those sixty

130
00:03:49,370 --> 00:03:51,727
billionths they were so excited about.

131
00:03:51,767 --> 00:03:54,579
Meanwhile, another experiment in the same Italian lab,

132
00:03:54,619 --> 00:03:56,885
ICARUS, timed the same particles:

133
00:03:56,925 --> 00:03:58,436
exactly the speed of light.

134
00:03:58,476 --> 00:04:00,204
Not faster, not slower.

135
00:04:00,749 --> 00:04:02,554
Cable fixed, math redone:

136
00:04:02,594 --> 00:04:03,906
the speed of light.

137
00:04:03,946 --> 00:04:05,637
Einstein wins.

138
00:04:05,677 --> 00:04:07,043
The leader had resigned.

139
00:04:07,083 --> 00:04:07,965
Who caught it, sir?

140
00:04:08,005 --> 00:04:08,888
Not a journalist.

141
00:04:08,928 --> 00:04:09,632
Not a government.

142
00:04:09,672 --> 00:04:11,015
Science itself.

143
00:04:11,055 --> 00:04:12,216
In under a year.

144
00:04:12,256 --> 00:04:14,955
There's a word humans misuse all the time:

145
00:04:14,995 --> 00:04:15,658
theory.

146
00:04:15,698 --> 00:04:18,090
On the street, a theory is a hunch.

147
00:04:18,130 --> 00:04:18,677
As in:

148
00:04:18,717 --> 00:04:21,455
I bet my neighbor steals my newspaper.

149
00:04:21,495 --> 00:04:23,803
But to scientists, the word means something else

150
00:04:23,843 --> 00:04:24,847
entirely.

151
00:04:24,887 --> 00:04:26,639
It starts as a hypothesis:

152
00:04:26,679 --> 00:04:27,944
a guess you can test.

153
00:04:27,984 --> 00:04:30,837
Tested a lot, survives, predicts things nobody has

154
00:04:30,877 --> 00:04:31,613
seen yet...

155
00:04:31,653 --> 00:04:33,841
only then is it a theory.

156
00:04:33,881 --> 00:04:35,644
A theory isn't the bottom of the ladder,

157
00:04:35,684 --> 00:04:36,105
sir.

158
00:04:36,145 --> 00:04:37,033
It's the top.

159
00:04:37,073 --> 00:04:37,809
Take gravity.

160
00:04:37,849 --> 00:04:39,262
Nobody calls it a hunch.

161
00:04:40,312 --> 00:04:42,176
Nobody steps off a roof to check.

162
00:04:42,780 --> 00:04:45,172
And that diseases come from tiny creatures you

163
00:04:45,212 --> 00:04:47,845
can't see, germs, is also a theory.

164
00:04:47,885 --> 00:04:49,809
It's why humans wash their hands.

165
00:04:49,849 --> 00:04:51,304
That one gets its own report.

166
00:04:52,559 --> 00:04:55,035
A harder test than explaining the past:

167
00:04:55,075 --> 00:04:56,876
predicting what will happen.

168
00:04:57,535 --> 00:05:00,556
In seventeen oh five, an Englishman, Edmond Halley,

169
00:05:00,596 --> 00:05:03,169
read old records of comets.

170
00:05:03,209 --> 00:05:05,611
He noticed one showed up about every seventy-six

171
00:05:05,651 --> 00:05:06,236
years.

172
00:05:06,276 --> 00:05:07,326
Not three comets.

173
00:05:07,366 --> 00:05:09,644
One comet, coming back.

174
00:05:09,684 --> 00:05:11,279
Using gravity, he said:

175
00:05:11,319 --> 00:05:14,351
it returns around seventeen fifty-eight.

176
00:05:14,391 --> 00:05:15,791
He died sixteen years before.

177
00:05:15,831 --> 00:05:18,019
And on Christmas night, seventeen fifty-eight...

178
00:05:18,059 --> 00:05:19,383
the comet appeared.

179
00:05:19,423 --> 00:05:20,513
It bears his name.

180
00:05:20,553 --> 00:05:22,652
Imagine signing a prediction you won't live to

181
00:05:22,692 --> 00:05:23,737
see.

182
00:05:23,777 --> 00:05:25,792
Crazy, or sure of your method.

183
00:05:25,832 --> 00:05:26,952
He wasn't crazy.

184
00:05:27,134 --> 00:05:27,742
Right.

185
00:05:27,782 --> 00:05:28,768
Back to the question:

186
00:05:28,808 --> 00:05:30,628
why do humans trust science?

187
00:05:30,668 --> 00:05:33,453
Not because scientists are smarter, or never wrong.

188
00:05:33,493 --> 00:05:35,548
They're wrong constantly, as we've seen.

189
00:05:35,636 --> 00:05:37,647
Humans trust it for two reasons.

190
00:05:37,863 --> 00:05:38,368
One:

191
00:05:38,408 --> 00:05:40,339
it's the only method built to hunt its

192
00:05:40,379 --> 00:05:41,583
own mistakes.

193
00:05:42,438 --> 00:05:43,918
Anyone can repeat the experiment.

194
00:05:43,958 --> 00:05:45,517
Prove the big names wrong, and you get

195
00:05:45,557 --> 00:05:47,007
a prize, not punishment.

196
00:05:47,047 --> 00:05:48,446
Not one scientist, sir.

197
00:05:48,486 --> 00:05:49,384
That's a mistake too.

198
00:05:49,424 --> 00:05:51,984
We trust many teams, in different countries, who

199
00:05:52,024 --> 00:05:54,457
dislike each other and chase the same prize,

200
00:05:54,497 --> 00:05:57,378
repeating the experiment and getting the same result.

201
00:05:57,883 --> 00:06:00,191
Then a conspiracy gets very hard.

202
00:06:00,231 --> 00:06:01,991
It needs everyone to agree.

203
00:06:02,145 --> 00:06:04,309
Scientists never agree on anything, sir...

204
00:06:04,349 --> 00:06:06,025
except results that repeat.

205
00:06:06,065 --> 00:06:06,863
Reason two:

206
00:06:06,903 --> 00:06:07,702
it works.

207
00:06:07,742 --> 00:06:09,840
The phone in your hand, the plane overhead,

208
00:06:09,880 --> 00:06:12,083
the bridge you're crossing.

209
00:06:12,123 --> 00:06:13,750
All scientific predictions.

210
00:06:13,790 --> 00:06:15,710
Humans bet their lives on them daily.

211
00:06:15,750 --> 00:06:17,405
And win.

212
00:06:17,445 --> 00:06:18,718
The Human Dictionary.

213
00:06:18,758 --> 00:06:19,623
Two words.

214
00:06:19,663 --> 00:06:20,637
Hypothesis:

215
00:06:20,677 --> 00:06:22,882
a testable guess, not yet proven.

216
00:06:22,922 --> 00:06:23,595
Theory:

217
00:06:23,635 --> 00:06:26,758
a hypothesis tested a thousand times that held.

218
00:06:26,798 --> 00:06:27,688
Not a hunch.

219
00:06:27,728 --> 00:06:28,598
Summary, sir.

220
00:06:28,638 --> 00:06:29,176
One:

221
00:06:29,216 --> 00:06:31,036
science isn't a book of answers.

222
00:06:31,076 --> 00:06:33,100
It's a method for not fooling yourself.

223
00:06:33,140 --> 00:06:33,582
Two:

224
00:06:33,622 --> 00:06:36,070
a scientific claim must be able to fail.

225
00:06:36,110 --> 00:06:37,609
Otherwise, it's a garage dragon.

226
00:06:37,649 --> 00:06:38,196
Three:

227
00:06:38,236 --> 00:06:41,099
scientists make mistakes, but the method catches them.

228
00:06:41,139 --> 00:06:43,431
Like the cable faster than light.

229
00:06:43,471 --> 00:06:44,228
I must admit:

230
00:06:44,268 --> 00:06:46,450
humans invented the only club on the planet

231
00:06:46,490 --> 00:06:49,362
that pays you to prove it wrong.

232
00:06:49,402 --> 00:06:50,138
Next report:

233
00:06:50,178 --> 00:06:52,297
how research is done, who checks it before

234
00:06:52,337 --> 00:06:54,655
it reaches you, and how one journalist fooled

235
00:06:54,695 --> 00:06:56,747
the world with chocolate.

236
00:06:56,787 --> 00:06:57,834
This was Earth Report.

237
00:06:57,874 --> 00:06:58,543
Agent Peek.

238
00:06:58,583 --> 00:06:59,687
End of transmission.
